1
00:00:17,700 --> 00:00:19,756
(pg.168) JM: then that bird looked back

2
00:00:19,756 --> 00:00:23,249
JP: alta uk kəlákəlá yaka nanich-k'ilapay

3
00:00:23,249 --> 00:00:26,464
JM: and he saw that in place of the boy there were only bones

4
00:00:26,464 --> 00:00:31,543
JP: alta yaka nanich-k'ilapay uk tənas-man <yaka təmtəm kʰəpit miməlust kʰupá>

5
00:00:31,543 --> 00:00:33,771
JM: they continued to go down the river (and Blue-Jay was quiet)

6
00:00:33,771 --> 00:00:41,255
JP: yaka ɬatwa alta yawa kʰupa ukuk hayas chək... ukuk kəlákələ hilu ikta yaka wawa

7
00:00:41,255 --> 00:00:44,033
JM: he looked back and the boy was sitting there again

8
00:00:44,033 --> 00:00:48,254
JP: yaka nanich k'ilapay yawa tənəs-man yaka miɬayt wəx̣t kʰupá

9
00:00:48,254 --> 00:00:52,005
JM: he said to him in a low voice

10
00:00:52,005 --> 00:00:54,758
JP: yaka wawa kʰupa yaka tənas-man

11
00:00:54,758 --> 00:00:56,876
JM: what is....where is your weir

12
00:00:56,876 --> 00:00:59,185
JP: qʰa ukuk alta <<mayka/mamuk/ukuk>> k'ilapay

13
00:00:59,185 --> 00:01:01,100
JM: he spoke slowly

14
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:03,006
JP: yaka wawa tunús wawa

15
00:01:03,006 --> 00:01:04,134
JM: the boy replied

16
00:01:04,134 --> 00:01:06,036
JP: ukuk tənas-man yaka wawa

17
00:01:06,036 --> 00:01:07,662
JM: it is down the river

18
00:01:07,662 --> 00:01:10,204
JP: yawa saya kikwəli kʰupa-ukuk chək

19
00:01:10,204 --> 00:01:11,886
JM: they went on

20
00:01:11,886 --> 00:01:13,438
JP: ɬaska ɬatwa yawa

21
00:01:13,438 --> 00:01:15,987
JM: then he said to him in a loud voice

22
00:01:15,987 --> 00:01:18,554
JP: alta yaka wawa kʰupa yaka hayash wawa

23
00:01:18,554 --> 00:01:20,398
JM: where is your weir

24
00:01:20,398 --> 00:01:22,480
JP: qʰa alta yax̣ka

25
00:01:22,480 --> 00:01:24,988
JM: and only a skeleton was there (in the stern of the canoe)

26
00:01:24,988 --> 00:01:28,170
JP: kʰəpit-ukuk ayaq tilay-miməlust alta kʰupá

27
00:01:28,170 --> 00:01:30,078
JM: the bird was silent again

28
00:01:30,078 --> 00:01:33,825
JP: ukuk kəlákəlá yaka hayaʔ... hilu-wawa wəx̣t

29
00:01:33,825 --> 00:01:36,578
JM: he looked back and the boy was there again

30
00:01:36,578 --> 00:01:40,438
JP: yaka nanich-k'ilapay pi uk tənəs-man wəx̣t yaka kʰupá

31
00:01:40,438 --> 00:01:42,441
JM: then he said in a low voice

32
00:01:42,445 --> 00:01:45,029
JP: yaka wawa kʰupa tənəs-wawa

33
00:01:45,029 --> 00:01:46,542
JM: where is your weir

34
00:01:46,542 --> 00:01:49,392
JP: qʰa ukuk alta <<yaka mayka uk kʰilta/kʰimt'a kapak'uk>>

35
00:01:49,392 --> 00:01:50,568
JM: "here" says the boy

36
00:01:50,568 --> 00:01:53,195
JP: yakwa yaka wawa tənəs-man

37
00:01:53,195 --> 00:01:55,848
JM: now they fished with their dipnets

38
00:01:55,848 --> 00:02:00,173
JP: alta ɬaska <<...>> alta ɬaska mamuk samən <<kʰupa ukuk>>  ɬaska dipnet

39
00:02:00,173 --> 00:02:03,466
JM: the bird felt something in his net

40
00:02:03,466 --> 00:02:07,292
JP: alta tənəs-man yaka ih...iskam ikta kʰupa yaka net

41
00:02:07,292 --> 00:02:09,280
JM: he lifted it

42
00:02:09,280 --> 00:02:10,882
JP: yaka mamuk ɬax̣

43
00:02:14,157 --> 00:02:17,661
JM: and he found only two branches in his net

44
00:02:17,661 --> 00:02:21,417
JP: yaka t'ɬap kʰopit ixt tənəs lima-stik kʰupa yaka net

45
00:02:21,417 --> 00:02:23,952
JM: he turned his net and threw it into the water

46
00:02:23,952 --> 00:02:26,437
JP: yaka mash yaka net wəx̣t kʰupa chək

47
00:02:28,379 --> 00:02:30,767
JM: it became full of leaves

48
00:02:30,767 --> 00:02:33,263
JP: yaka chaku-pʰaɬ ukuk kʰanawi

49
00:02:34,091 --> 00:02:37,130
JM: he turned his net and threw them into the water

50
00:02:37,130 --> 00:02:40,997
JP: yaka mamuk-k'ilapay yaka net pi yaka mash wəx̣t kʰupa chək

51
00:02:40,997 --> 00:02:44,099
JM: but part of the leaves fell into the canoe

52
00:02:44,099 --> 00:02:48,470
JP: bət <<sitkum ukuk, sitkum ukuk>> nawitka kʰupá kənim

53
00:02:50,318 --> 00:02:52,493
JM: the boy gathered them up

54
00:02:52,493 --> 00:02:55,180
JP: ukuk tənəs-man yaka iskam kʰanawi

55
00:02:55,180 --> 00:02:57,919
JM: then another branch came into the bird's net

56
00:02:57,919 --> 00:03:01,151
JP: alta wəx̣t uk ɬatwa t'ɬux̣ kʰupa ukuk yaka net

57
00:03:01,151 --> 00:03:03,424
JM: he turned the net and threw it into the water

58
00:03:03,424 --> 00:03:06,451
JP: yaka mamuk-k'ilapay ukuk pi yaka mash kʰupa chək

59
00:03:06,451 --> 00:03:10,051
JM: some leaves came into it and he threw it into the water

60
00:03:10,051 --> 00:03:15,013
JP: <<pi>> ukuk chaku wəx̣t kʰupa ukuk <<kʰupá/ikta/kənim>> pi yaka mash ɬaska kʰupa chək

61
00:03:15,013 --> 00:03:17,340
JM: all of the leaves fell into the canoe

62
00:03:17,340 --> 00:03:19,947
 JP: <<wəx̣t/ikta/alta uk(uk)>> yaka t'ɬux̣ kʰupá ukuk kənim

63
00:03:19,947 --> 00:03:21,916
JM: the boy gathered them up

64
00:03:21,916 --> 00:03:24,876
JP: ukuk tənəs-man yaka iskam ukuk kʰanawi

65
00:03:27,676 --> 00:03:30,794
JM: the bird was pleased with two of the branches

66
00:03:30,794 --> 00:03:34,765
JP: ukuk tənəs-man yaka ɬush-təmtəm yaka makwst tənəs lima-stik

67
00:03:34,765 --> 00:03:36,569
JM: "I will carry them home" he said 

68
00:03:36,569 --> 00:03:39,128
JP: <<alta/qʰa>> ɬaska nayka <<mamuk uk q'oʔ>> kʰupa <<nayka/ukuk>> haws

69
00:03:39,128 --> 00:03:41,265
JM: she may use them for making fire

70
00:03:41,265 --> 00:03:44,043
JP: alax̣ti yaka mamuk paya ɬaska ukuk

71
00:03:44,043 --> 00:03:46,367
JM: these branches branches were large

72
00:03:46,367 --> 00:03:49,194
JP: wəx̣t alta yaka təmtəm lima-stik ɬaska ɬush

73
00:03:49,194 --> 00:03:51,460
JM: they arrived at home and went up to the house

74
00:03:51,460 --> 00:03:54,467
JP: yaka iskam wəx̣t <<pi>> yaka ɬatwa kʰupa yaka haws

75
00:03:54,467 --> 00:03:55,974
JM: the bird was angry

76
00:03:55,974 --> 00:03:58,938
JP: ukuk tənəs-man ukuk kəlákəlá tənəs-saliks

77
00:03:58,938 --> 00:04:00,935
JM: because he had not caught anything

78
00:04:00,935 --> 00:04:03,739
JP: because he... pus hilu-ikta yaka iskam

79
00:04:03,739 --> 00:04:06,877
JM: they boy brought a mat full of trout up to the house

80
00:04:06,877 --> 00:04:11,452
JP: uk tənəs-man yaka mamuk-k'ilapay pʰaɬ ɬiskwis kʰupa yaka haws

81
00:04:11,452 --> 00:04:13,653
JM: and the people roasted them

82
00:04:13,653 --> 00:04:18,117
JP: ukuk tilixam ɬaska paya ɬaska ukuk <<məkʰmək/makuk>> paya-stik

83
00:04:21,008 --> 00:04:22,786
JM: then the boy told them

84
00:04:22,786 --> 00:04:26,697
JP: ukuk tənəs.. ukuk tənəs-man yaka wawa kʰupa yaka

85
00:04:26,697 --> 00:04:29,833
JM: he threw out of the canoe what we had caught

86
00:04:29,833 --> 00:04:33,309
JP: ikta mayka mash kʰupa ukuk kənim ɬax̣ani yaka iskam

87
00:04:33,309 --> 00:04:36,841
JM: our canoe would have been full if he had not thrown it away

88
00:04:36,841 --> 00:04:40,201
JP: ukuk kənim yaka pʰaɬ pus wik yaka mash ɬax̣ani

89
00:04:40,201 --> 00:04:41,942
JM: his sister said to him

90
00:04:41,942 --> 00:04:44,440
JP: pi yaka ats yaka wawa kʰupa yax̣ka

91
00:04:44,440 --> 00:04:46,648
JM: why did you throw away what you had caught?

92
00:04:46,648 --> 00:04:49,548
JP: qʰata <<ikta/uk>> mayka mash ukuk ikta mayka iskam

93
00:04:49,548 --> 00:04:52,654
JM: I threw it away because we had caught nothing but branches

94
00:04:52,654 --> 00:04:57,152
JP: nayka mash ukuk pus hilu-ikta mamuk iskam.  kʰəpit ukuk lima-stik.

95
00:04:57,152 --> 00:04:58,912
JM: "that is our food" she said

96
00:04:58,912 --> 00:05:00,900
JP: kʰupá ukuk nsayka məkmək

97
00:05:00,900 --> 00:05:02,722
JM: do you think they were leaves?

98
00:05:02,722 --> 00:05:05,340
JP: nayka munk-təmtəm kʰəltəs-ikta ukuk

99
00:05:05,340 --> 00:05:07,212
JM: those leaves were trout

100
00:05:07,212 --> 00:05:10,012
JP: ukuk...ukuk ikta yaka tənəs-samən

101
00:05:10,012 --> 00:05:11,899
JM: the branches salmon

102
00:05:11,899 --> 00:05:14,682
JP: ukuk tənəs lima-stik yaka samən

103
00:05:14,682 --> 00:05:16,210
JM: he said to his sister

104
00:05:16,210 --> 00:05:18,195
JP: yaka wawa kʰupa yaka ats

105
00:05:18,195 --> 00:05:20,169
JM: I brought you two branches

106
00:05:20,169 --> 00:05:23,678
JP: nayka lulú kʰupa ma..mayka makwst lima-stik

107
00:05:23,678 --> 00:05:25,946
JM: you may use them for making fire

108
00:05:25,946 --> 00:05:28,135
JP: mayka mamuk paya kʰupa ukuk

109
00:05:28,135 --> 00:05:30,233
JM: then his sister went down to the beach

110
00:05:30,233 --> 00:05:33,755
JP: alta yaka ats yaka ɬatwa yawa kʰupa ukuk tən-chək

111
00:05:33,755 --> 00:05:37,028
JM: now there were two salmon in the canoe

112
00:05:37,028 --> 00:05:41,100
JP: alta kʰupá makwst samən ukuk kənim.. kʰupa ukuk kənim

113
00:05:41,100 --> 00:05:42,986
JM: she carried them up to the house

114
00:05:42,986 --> 00:05:45,713
JP: alta yaka lulu kʰupa ukuk kʰupa haws

115
00:05:45,713 --> 00:05:47,910
JM: and she enters carrying them in her hands

116
00:05:47,910 --> 00:05:52,067
JP: yaka q'uʔ yaka ɬatwa haws ukuk yaka mamuk-k'ilapay kʰupa yaka lima

117
00:05:52,067 --> 00:05:53,363
JM: then the bird said

118
00:05:53,363 --> 00:05:55,408
JP: alta kəlákələ yaka wawa

119
00:05:55,408 --> 00:05:57,213
JM: where did you steal these salmon?

120
00:05:57,213 --> 00:06:00,445
JP: kʰupa qʰa mayka iskam ukuk samən kapshwala

121
00:06:00,445 --> 00:06:02,319
JM: "that is what you caught" she said

122
00:06:02,319 --> 00:06:04,208
JP: ukuk ikta mayka iskam

123
00:06:04,208 --> 00:06:05,995
JM: my sister is lying

124
00:06:05,995 --> 00:06:08,630
JP: nayka ats yaka t'ɬəminxwət kʰupa mayka

125
00:06:08,630 --> 00:06:10,997
JM: on the next day the bird went to the beach

126
00:06:10,997 --> 00:06:14,840
JP: iwa wəx̣t san ukuk kəlákəlá yaka ɬatwa <yawa> kʰupa chək

127
00:06:14,840 --> 00:06:17,859
JM: there lay the canoe of the ghosts

128
00:06:17,859 --> 00:06:23,594
JP: qʰa uk kʰupá yaka miɬayt ukuk kənim ukuk <<anqati/qʰata>> miməlust tilixam yaka kənim

129
00:06:23,594 --> 00:06:28,631
JM: they had all holes and part of them were mossgrown

130
00:06:28,631 --> 00:06:37,122
JP: kənim ɬaska mmm..hilu... pʰaɬ-ɬxwap ɬq'up-ɬq'up. ɬaska miɬayt ukuk hayu-ikta yaka chaku-hayash kʰupá

131
00:06:37,122 --> 00:06:41,381
JM: he went to the house and said to his sister

132
00:06:41,381 --> 00:06:45,318
JP: yaka ɬatwa yawa kʰupa haws. yaka wawa kʰupa yaka ats

133
00:06:45,318 --> 00:06:48,487
JM: your husband's canoes are bad

134
00:06:48,487 --> 00:06:54,555
JP: ukuk mayka (cough)...ukuk mayka man yaka kənim...mashachi alta

135
00:06:54,555 --> 00:06:56,324
JM: "be quiet" she said

136
00:06:56,324 --> 00:06:58,960
JP: nayka uk nayka wik-ikta wawa yaka wawa

137
00:06:58,960 --> 00:07:01,130
JM: the people will become tired of you

138
00:07:01,130 --> 00:07:04,677
JP: ukuk tilixam ɬaska mitxwit chaku tʰil kʰupa mayka

139
00:07:04,677 --> 00:07:08,212
JM: the canoes of these people are full of holes

140
00:07:08,212 --> 00:07:12,178
JP: wəx̣t ukuk kənim ukuk tilixam ɬq'up-pʰaɬ ɬxwap

141
00:07:12,178 --> 00:07:14,043
JM: then his sister said to him

142
00:07:14,043 --> 00:07:16,722
JP: alta yaka ats yaka wawa kʰupa yaka

143
00:07:16,722 --> 00:07:18,040
JM: are they people?

144
00:07:18,040 --> 00:07:21,497
JP: yaka m... ɬaska tili...tilixam....kʰanawi?

145
00:07:21,497 --> 00:07:22,836
JM: they are ghosts

146
00:07:22,836 --> 00:07:25,847
JP: alta kʰupa miməlust tilixam ukuk bons ɬaska

147
00:07:25,847 --> 00:07:27,393
JM: it grew dark again

148
00:07:27,393 --> 00:07:29,490
JP: alta yaka chaku pulakʰli

149
00:07:29,490 --> 00:07:31,440
JM: and the bird made himself ready

150
00:07:31,440 --> 00:07:33,869
JP: alta uk kəlákəlá yaka mamuk-ɬush

151
00:07:33,869 --> 00:07:37,042
JM: and the boy made himself ready also

152
00:07:37,042 --> 00:07:40,497
JP: alta tənas man yaka mamuk ɬush wəx̣t

153
00:07:40,497 --> 00:07:42,826
JM: they went again

154
00:07:42,826 --> 00:07:44,701
JP: alta wəx̣t ɬaska ɬatwa

155
00:07:44,701 --> 00:07:47,133
JM: now he sees the boy

156
00:07:47,133 --> 00:07:49,453
JP: alta yaka nanich ukuk tənas-man

157
00:07:49,453 --> 00:07:52,013
JM: when they were on their way he shouted

158
00:07:52,013 --> 00:07:55,515
JP: yaka ɬatwa kʰəltəs-saya ɬaska p'uʔ yaka

159
00:07:55,515 --> 00:07:57,372
JM: and only bones were there

160
00:07:57,372 --> 00:07:59,917
JP: kʰəpit ukuk yaka bons <alta> kʰupá

161
00:07:59,917 --> 00:08:04,069
JM: he did this several times before he arrived

162
00:08:04,069 --> 00:08:09,748
JP: yaka mamuk alta qʰənchi-hayu... <<iləp pus>> k'ilapay... q'u kʰupá

163
00:08:09,748 --> 00:08:12,089
JM: now they fished with a dipnet

164
00:08:12,089 --> 00:08:16,707
JP: alta ɬaska miɬayt  <<kʰupá ɬatwa  q'uʔ anɬq>> ɬaska mamuk samən ukuk ɬaska dipnet

165
00:08:16,707 --> 00:08:22,145
JM: he gathered the branches and the leaves which he had caught

166
00:08:22,145 --> 00:08:28,401
JP: alta ɬaska iskam ukuk kʰanawi <<ukuk>> ɬaska iskam lima-stik kʰanawi-ikta

167
00:08:28,401 --> 00:08:31,535
JM: <<and on the inside>> in their canoe was full

168
00:08:31,535 --> 00:08:36,056
JP: qʰənchi ukuk hayash chək chaku-k'ilapay ɬaska <<uk>> kənim pʰatɬ alta

169
00:08:36,056 --> 00:08:37,642
JM: then they went home

170
00:08:37,642 --> 00:08:40,411
JP: alta ɬaska k'ilapay kʰupa ɬaska haws

171
00:08:40,411 --> 00:08:42,990
JM: now he sees the door

172
00:08:42,990 --> 00:08:47,369
JP: alta yaka nanich.. ukuk...<<?>> alta miməlust tilixam ya kənim

173
00:08:47,369 --> 00:08:50,374
JM: he shouted as soon as they met one

174
00:08:50,374 --> 00:08:54,287
JP: ɬaska yaka kʰriye <<kʰupa>> ɬaska nanich ixt

175
00:08:54,287 --> 00:08:56,260
JM: and only bones could be seen

176
00:08:56,260 --> 00:08:58,879
JP: kʰəpit ukuk yaka bons ɬaska nanich

177
00:08:58,879 --> 00:09:00,753
JM: they arrived at home

178
00:09:00,753 --> 00:09:03,012
JP: ɬaska q'uʔ kʰupa yaka haws

179
00:09:03,012 --> 00:09:04,846
JM: he went up to his sister

180
00:09:04,846 --> 00:09:06,972
JP: yaka ɬatwa kʰupa yaka ats

181
00:09:06,972 --> 00:09:09,435
JM: he carried up the salmon which he had caught

182
00:09:09,435 --> 00:09:13,654
JP: yaka mamuk-q'uʔ yakwa uk samən ɬaska iskam mamuk-chaku

183
00:09:13,654 --> 00:09:16,413
JM: the next morning

184
00:09:16,413 --> 00:09:17,050
JP: <<ukuk>>

185
00:09:17,050 --> 00:09:18,877
JM: the bird went into the town

186
00:09:18,877 --> 00:09:21,439
JP: ukuk kəlákələ yaka ɬatwa yawa kʰupa tʰawn

187
00:09:21,439 --> 00:09:25,136
JM: he found many bones in the house

188
00:09:25,136 --> 00:09:27,575
JP: yaka t'ɬap hayu bon kʰupa yaka haws

189
00:09:27,575 --> 00:09:30,412
JM: when it grew dark, somebody said

190
00:09:30,412 --> 00:09:33,738
JP: yaka chaku pulakʰli, ɬaksta yaka wawa

191
00:09:33,738 --> 00:09:36,228
JM: a whale has been found

192
00:09:36,228 --> 00:09:39,151
JP: ukuk hayash samən ɬaska iskam

193
00:09:39,151 --> 00:09:42,229
JM: his sister gave him a knife and said to him

194
00:09:42,229 --> 00:09:46,471
JP: ukuk yaka ats palach yaka uptsax̣ <<pi>> yaka wawa kʰupa yax̣ka

195
00:09:46,471 --> 00:09:49,026
JM: run, a whale has been found

196
00:09:49,026 --> 00:09:54,178
JP: kuri...kuri-ayaq, <<...>> hayash samən ɬaska iskam

197
00:09:54,178 --> 00:09:56,522
JM: the bird ran and came to the beach

198
00:09:56,522 --> 00:10:01,754
JP: ukuk kəlakəla yaka ɬax̣...kuli yawa kʰupa <<ukuk/mamuk>> saya kʰupa hayash-chək

199
00:10:01,754 --> 00:10:03,994
JM: he met one of the people

200
00:10:03,994 --> 00:10:06,877
JP: yaka ɬatwa <<<məʔux̣>>>..ixt tilixam

201
00:10:06,877 --> 00:10:08,821
JM: speaking loudly he asked

202
00:10:08,821 --> 00:10:10,949
JP: yaka wawa

203
00:10:10,949 --> 00:10:13,111
JM: where is that whale?

204
00:10:13,111 --> 00:10:15,644
JP: ikta... qʰa ukuk hayash samən?

205
00:10:15,644 --> 00:10:17,749
JM: only a skeleton layed there

206
00:10:17,749 --> 00:10:20,613
JP: kʰəpit ukuk bon yaka miɬayt kʰupá

207
00:10:20,613 --> 00:10:22,855
JM: he kicked the skull and left it

208
00:10:22,855 --> 00:10:27,307
JP: yaka iskam ukuk yaka... miməlust yaka latet yaka <<lulu/luʔluʔ>>

209
00:10:27,307 --> 00:10:30,497
JM: he went some distance and met other people

210
00:10:30,497 --> 00:10:35,603
JP: yaka kuri ukuk tənəs-saya pi yaka ɬatwa x̣luyma tilixam

211
00:10:35,603 --> 00:10:37,104
JM: he shouted loudly

212
00:10:37,104 --> 00:10:39,311
JP: yaka kʰriye tənəs-hayash

213
00:10:39,311 --> 00:10:41,458
JM: only skeletons lay there

214
00:10:41,458 --> 00:10:44,986
JP: kʰəpit ukuk yaka bon-miməlust-tilixam miɬayt kʰupá

215
00:10:44,986 --> 00:10:48,892
JM: several times he acted this way towards the people

216
00:10:48,892 --> 00:10:51,914
JP: qʰənchi yaka hayu-mamuk kakwa <<k'oʔ/kʰupa>> kʰupá

217
00:10:51,914 --> 00:10:53,848
JM: then he came to a large log

218
00:10:53,848 --> 00:10:58,431
JP: <<mayka>> mamuk <<k'oʔ> kʰupá ukuk hayash stik <<mamuk kəmtəks ukuk>>

219
00:10:58,431 --> 00:11:00,375
JM: it's bark was very thick

220
00:11:00,375 --> 00:11:04,463
JP: ukuk...<<ulhəm>> yaka skin dret hayash

221
00:11:04,463 --> 00:11:07,429
JM: there was a crowd of people who peeled off the bark

222
00:11:07,429 --> 00:11:11,176
JP: hayu tilixam ɬaska munk-ɬaq-ɬaq ukuk yaka skin

223
00:11:11,176 --> 00:11:13,028
JM: the bird shouted

224
00:11:13,028 --> 00:11:15,046
JP: ukuk kəlákələ yaka kʰriye

225
00:11:15,046 --> 00:11:16,977
JM: only skeletons lay there

226
00:11:16,977 --> 00:11:20,463
JP: kʰəpit ukuk yaka bon miməlust-tilixam miɬayt kʰupá

227
00:11:20,463 --> 00:11:22,441
JM: the bark was full of pitch

228
00:11:22,441 --> 00:11:29,270
JP: ukuk...ukuk uhh...hayash stik yaka skin kʰupit-kʰanawi <<uh/ya>> pʰatɬ-lakum

229
00:11:29,270 --> 00:11:31,000
JM: he peeled off two pieces

230
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:32,869
JP: yaka mamuk-ɬaq makwst

231
00:11:32,869 --> 00:11:35,381
JM: he carried them on his shoulder and went home

232
00:11:35,381 --> 00:11:40,368
JP: yaka lulu ɬas<ka> kʰupa yaka shoulder yaka lulu hom, yaka lulu kʰupa yaka haws

233
00:11:40,368 --> 00:11:41,485
JM: he thought

234
00:11:41,485 --> 00:11:42,857
JP: yaka təmtəm

235
00:11:42,857 --> 00:11:45,195
JM: I really believed it is a whale

236
00:11:45,195 --> 00:11:50,442
JP: nayka <kə...> dilet kəmtəks ya təmtəm alta ya wawa uk hayash samən miɬayt

237
00:11:50,442 --> 00:11:53,828
JM: when he arrived he put down the bark outside the house

238
00:11:53,828 --> 00:12:00,000
JP: yaka q'uʔ kʰupa yaka haws yaka mash ukuk yaka skin ya... stik-skin yawa ɬax̣ani

239
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:01,335
JM: he said to his sister

240
00:12:01,335 --> 00:12:03,148
JP: yaka wawa kʰupa yaka ats

241
00:12:03,148 --> 00:12:05,194
JM: I thought it was a whale

242
00:12:05,194 --> 00:12:07,638
JP: nayka təmtəm ukuk yaka hayash samən

243
00:12:07,638 --> 00:12:09,376
JM: look here it is bark

244
00:12:09,376 --> 00:12:12,491
JP: nanich ukuk <<yakwa...>> lakum-stik yaka skin

245
00:12:12,491 --> 00:12:13,922
JM: it is whale meat

246
00:12:13,922 --> 00:12:16,493
JP: yax̣ka ukuk hayash samən yaka iɬwəli

247
00:12:16,493 --> 00:12:18,083
JM: do you think it is bark?

248
00:12:18,083 --> 00:12:21,085
JP: mayka mamuk-təmtəm ukuk yaka stik-skin

249
00:12:21,085 --> 00:12:25,961
JM: his sister went out and two cuts of whale lay on the ground

250
00:12:25,961 --> 00:12:32,627
JP: yaka... yaka ats yaka ɬatwa ɬax̣ani yaka nanich ukuk makwst <ɬq'up-ɬq'up> kʰupa hayash samən yaka <skin> yawa

251
00:12:32,627 --> 00:12:34,184
JM: it is a good whale

252
00:12:34,184 --> 00:12:37,174
JP: yaka hayash ɬush ukuk hayash samən yakwa

253
00:12:37,174 --> 00:12:38,747
JM: it is very thick

254
00:12:38,747 --> 00:12:40,528
JP: hayash hayash

255
00:12:40,528 --> 00:12:45,609
JM: then the bird looked and there lay a whale on the beach

256
00:12:45,609 --> 00:12:50,462
JP: alta yaka nanich ukuk kəlákələ yaka nanich hayash samən yawa miɬayt

257
00:12:50,462 --> 00:12:51,866
JM: he turned back

258
00:12:51,866 --> 00:12:53,283
JP: yaka k'ilapay

259
00:12:53,283 --> 00:12:56,624
JM: he met a person carrying bark on his back

260
00:12:56,624 --> 00:13:01,843
JP: yaka <ya> nanich uk tilixam lulu skin-stik kʰupa <yaka> shoulder yawa

261
00:13:01,843 --> 00:13:05,631
JM: he shouted and nothing but a skeleton lay there

262
00:13:05,631 --> 00:13:10,789
JP: yaka kʰriye <pi> hilu-ikta kʰupit <uk tilix...> miməlust-tilixam yaka bons kʰupá

263
00:13:10,789 --> 00:13:14,034
JM: he took that piece of bark and carried it home on his shoulder

264
00:13:14,034 --> 00:13:19,734
JP: yaka lulu-iskam ukuk wəx̣t ɬq'up-skin-stik yaka lulu kʰupa yaka haws

265
00:13:19,734 --> 00:13:23,277
JM: this he did to all of the ghosts

266
00:13:23,277 --> 00:13:28,846
JP: kakwa yaka mamuk <<mamuk məkʰmək>> ukuk <<anqati/alta>> miməlust-tilixam yaka lulu ɬatwa kʰupá

267
00:13:28,846 --> 00:13:31,646
JM: in the course of time he had much whale meat

268
00:13:31,646 --> 00:13:38,059
JP: << aɬqi alta miməlust-aɬqi <alta/yaka> kənim hayu kʰupit-ixt yax̣ka yaka iskam ukuk <iɬwəli/...hayu> >>

269
00:13:38,059 --> 00:13:40,428
JM: now he continued to stay there

270
00:13:40,428 --> 00:13:43,169
JP: alta yaka miɬayt kʰupá kwansəm

271
00:13:43,169 --> 00:13:45,504
JM: he went again to that town

272
00:13:45,504 --> 00:13:49,961
JP: alta wəx̣t yaka ɬatwa ukuk hayash tʰawn.. o...hayu haws

273
00:13:49,961 --> 00:13:53,524
JM: he entered a house and took this child's skull

274
00:13:53,524 --> 00:14:00,874
JP: yaka iskam kʰupa ixt... ɬatwa kʰupa ixt haws, yawa yaka iskam ukuk tənas yaka latet

275
00:14:00,874 --> 00:14:03,241
JM: which he put on a large skeleton

276
00:14:03,241 --> 00:14:07,620
JP: yaka mamuk miɬayt kʰupa hayash bons... mimelust tilixam yaka bons

277
00:14:07,620 --> 00:14:10,620
JM: he took a large skull and he put on a child's skeleton

278
00:14:10,620 --> 00:14:16,944
JP: yaka iskam hayash tilixam yaka latet pi <yaka mamuk-miɬayt yawa kʰupa miməlust-tənas tilixam yaka> latet

279
00:14:16,944 --> 00:14:18,720
JM: this he did to all the people

280
00:14:18,720 --> 00:14:21,472
JP: kakwa yaka mamuk <<kʰupa>> kʰanawi ukuk tilixam

281
00:14:21,472 --> 00:14:25,597
JM: when it grew dark the child rose to speak

282
00:14:25,597 --> 00:14:30,592
JP: qʰənchi yaka chaku pulakʰli ukuk tənas yaka mash kʰupa-ukuk hayu-wawa

283
00:14:34,157 --> 00:14:35,877
JM: it wanted to sit up

284
00:14:35,877 --> 00:14:37,920
JP: yaka tiki miɬayt

285
00:14:37,920 --> 00:14:42,748
JM: so it fell down again because it's head pulled it down

286
00:14:42,748 --> 00:14:47,348
JP: yaka t'ɬux̣ wəx̣t ukuk pus yaka latit mamuk-hal yax̣ka ikta

287
00:14:47,348 --> 00:14:49,323
JM: the old man rose

288
00:14:49,323 --> 00:14:51,868
JP: ukuk ulman yaka miɬayt

289
00:14:51,868 --> 00:14:53,639
JM: his head was light

290
00:14:53,639 --> 00:14:57,642
JP: yaka latit <wəx̣t... x̣um/wax̣... x̣um>

291
00:14:57,642 --> 00:15:01,959
JM: the next morning they replaced the head

292
00:15:01,959 --> 00:15:06,660
JP: <ukuk> tənəs san <yaka mamuk>-miɬayt k'ilapay uk latet

293
00:15:06,660 --> 00:15:09,888
JM: sometimes he did this to the legs of the ghost

294
00:15:09,888 --> 00:15:14,153
JP: kakupus <uk...> kakupus yaka mamuk <mamuk> miməlust tilixam kwansəm

295
00:15:14,153 --> 00:15:17,704
JM: he gave small legs to <old/tall> people

296
00:15:17,704 --> 00:15:23,364
JP: yaka paɬlach...yaka paɬlach ukuk hayash lipʰyi kʰupa hayash tilixam

297
00:15:23,364 --> 00:15:25,820
JM: and large legs to a small child

298
00:15:25,820 --> 00:15:29,519
JP: pi hayu hayash lipʰyi kʰupa <ˈtənás-tənás>

299
00:15:29,519 --> 00:15:33,754
JM: sometimes he exchanged a man's and a woman's legs

300
00:15:33,754 --> 00:15:40,145
JP: ixt-ixt pus yaka mamuk... mamuk x̣luyma ukuk ɬuchmən yaka lipʰyi

301
00:15:40,145 --> 00:15:43,151
JM: in the course of time they began to dislike him

302
00:15:43,151 --> 00:15:48,084
JP: ukuk alta tənəs yax̣ka tənəs-lili alta uk tilixam wik-tiki yax̣ka

303
00:15:59,084 --> 00:16:01,454
JM: tell him he shall go home they said

304
00:16:01,454 --> 00:16:04,870
JP: alta ɬaska wawa ɬuchmən ya ɬatwa-haws alta

305
00:16:04,870 --> 00:16:09,609
JM: that is what his brother-in-law said to his sister

306
00:16:09,609 --> 00:16:12,907
JP: <kakwa ukuk yaka wawa yaka aw kʰupa yaka ats>

307
00:16:12,907 --> 00:16:15,543
JM: on the next morning he awoke early

308
00:16:15,543 --> 00:16:20,723
JP: <ukuk wəx̣t/ixt> tənəs-san .... <yaka/mayka>....mayka miɬayt tənəs-san

309
00:16:20,723 --> 00:16:26,637
JM: now his sister held a skull in her arms

310
00:16:26,637 --> 00:16:30,626
JP: alta ukuk yaka ats yaka <... kʰupa yaka ...>

311
00:16:30,626 --> 00:16:31,939
JM: he threw it away

312
00:16:31,939 --> 00:16:33,409
JP: yaka mash yawa

313
00:16:33,409 --> 00:16:35,894
JM: why did you hold that old skull again

314
00:16:35,894 --> 00:16:39,772
JP: qʰata <mamuk ukuk> mayka iskam ukuk miməlust tilixam yaka latet

315
00:16:39,772 --> 00:16:42,674
JM: you broke your brother-in-law's neck

316
00:16:42,674 --> 00:16:45,988
JP: mayka mamuk <kakshət/kashin> ukuk mayka aw yaka liku

317
00:16:45,988 --> 00:16:47,154
JM: it grew dark

318
00:16:47,154 --> 00:16:48,657
JP: yaka chaku pulakʰli

319
00:16:48,657 --> 00:16:51,011
JM: now his brother-in-law was sick

320
00:16:51,011 --> 00:16:53,514
JP: alta yaka aw yaka hayash sik

321
00:16:53,514 --> 00:16:56,801
JM: the wom...the man tried to cure himself

322
00:16:56,801 --> 00:16:59,659
JP: kakupus ixt man yaka tiki mamuk ɬush yaka

323
00:16:59,659 --> 00:17:01,005
JM: he became alive again

324
00:17:01,005 --> 00:17:02,529
JP: yaka chaku ɬush

325
00:17:02,529 --> 00:17:05,676
JM: now the boy went home

326
00:17:05,676 --> 00:17:08,223
JP: alta ukuk kəlákəlá yaka ɬatwa kʰupa yaka haws

327
00:17:08,223 --> 00:17:11,801
JM: his sister gave him five buckets full of water

328
00:17:11,801 --> 00:17:17,866
JP: yaka ats paɬlach yaka kitɬən, qwinəm ukuk, chək...hmm chək

329
00:17:17,866 --> 00:17:20,592
JM: and he told him

330
00:17:20,592 --> 00:17:22,005
JP: wawa kʰupa yax̣ka

331
00:17:22,005 --> 00:17:23,156
JM: take care

332
00:17:23,156 --> 00:17:24,748
JP: mayka ɬush-nanich

333
00:17:24,748 --> 00:17:29,357
JM: when you come to the burning prairies

334
00:17:29,357 --> 00:17:34,117
JP: mayka chaku kʰupa ukuk, alta, ukuk paya ɬush-iliʔi

335
00:17:34,117 --> 00:17:37,436
JM: do not pour it out til you come to the fourth prairie

336
00:17:37,436 --> 00:17:43,180
JP: mayka mamuk-wax̣ pus mayka chaku ukuk (on) paya... ɬush-iliʔi

337
00:17:43,180 --> 00:17:44,656
JM: then pour it out

338
00:17:44,656 --> 00:17:47,155
JP: alta mayka mash ukuk mayka chək kʰupá

339
00:17:47,155 --> 00:17:49,810
JM: now he went

340
00:17:49,810 --> 00:17:51,195
JP: alta yaka ɬatwa

341
00:17:51,195 --> 00:17:52,730
JM: he reached the prairie

342
00:17:52,730 --> 00:17:55,514
JP: yaka q'uʔ kʰupa ukuk hayash ɬush-iliʔi

343
00:17:55,514 --> 00:18:01,084
JM: it was hot

344
00:18:01,084 --> 00:18:03,249
JP: hayash wam yaka kʰupá

345
00:18:03,249 --> 00:18:07,078
JM: red flowers bloomed on the prairie

346
00:18:07,078 --> 00:18:12,734
JP: <...> ukuk ukuk ukuk pʰil t'səm <<qʰa yaka chaku s..>> chaku kʰupá

347
00:18:12,734 --> 00:18:17,374
JM: they poured water on

348
00:18:17,374 --> 00:18:22,223
JP: yaka mamuk-wax̣... yaka mamuk-wax̣ kʰupa ukuk chək kʰupá

349
00:18:22,223 --> 00:18:24,837
JM: one of his buckets was half empty

350
00:18:24,837 --> 00:18:31,413
JP: ukuk kit... ukuk ixt yaka kitɬən wik...wik saya...katsaq

351
00:18:31,413 --> 00:18:34,011
JM: he leaves the woods

352
00:18:34,011 --> 00:18:36,418
JP: yaka <ɬax̣> kʰupa ukuk stik <<iliʔi>>

353
00:18:36,418 --> 00:18:41,637
JM: and soon he came to another prairie

354
00:18:41,637 --> 00:18:45,050
JP: wik lili wəx̣t yaka chaku kʰapuk tənəs-ɬush-iliʔi

355
00:18:48,235 --> 00:18:50,804
JM: which was burning at it's end

356
00:18:50,804 --> 00:18:53,994
JP: yaka wəx̣t kʰupa ukuk hayash paya kʰupá

357
00:18:53,994 --> 00:18:56,704
JM: he reached another prairie which was half on fire

358
00:18:56,704 --> 00:19:01,653
JP: pi wəx̣t q'uʔ <wəx̣t> kʰupa ixt tənəs-ɬush-iliʔi wəx̣t kʰupa paya

